 "title": "Why Your Second Job Should Be Making Content",
 "topic": "content creation",
 "summary": "The creator argues that everyone's second job should be making content because distribution matters more than pure competence in business today. Brands and publishers prefer backing people with an existing audience over more competent individuals with no followers.",
 "key_points": [
  "No matter what your main job is, your second job should be to make content.",
  "The creator shares a clip of Scott Galloway, who explains that publishers give big book advances because they look at a creator's followers and know they will sell a certain amount regardless of the book's quality.",
  "Scott Galloway is a non-fiction author in the US with 1.8 million Instagram followers on his profile profgalloway.",
  "The creator claims that in 2026, it is not about who is more competent, but who has bigger distribution.",
  "Economically, it makes complete sense for brands, companies, and publishers to bet on someone with an existing audience instead of someone who might be more competent but has no buyers.",
  "The creator states that founders will see more creator-entrepreneurs funded by big seasoned entrepreneurs or brands in the future.",
  "The creator advises founders or coaches who are delaying building a distribution on Instagram to check out their page and talk in the DM."
 ],
 "content": "Find your 2nd job. Do you think Ashneer Grover is a great author, or these two people are more talented or skilled than you? We both know the answer and that is why I say no matter what your job is, your second job should be to make content. And I will tell you exactly why, but first watch this clip. [Scott Galloway clip:] That is they know I have enough I have enough of a background to know I will write a competent book. Mhm. But the reason they think they're willing to give me a big advance is they look at my followers and go, he's going to he's going to sell a he could put out crap and he'll sell a certain amount because he has a big following. For people who don't know, Scott Galloway is one of the biggest nonfiction authors in the US, and he says publishers give him more money just because he has got an Instagram following of 1.8 million followers. And that is the literal cheat code in 2026 because it's not about who is more competent, it is about who has bigger distribution. And I know it's unfortunate and you can complain all about it, I do as well, but economically, it just makes complete sense for brands, for companies, or for publishers to bet on someone who has got followers behind him, instead of someone who might be more competent, but has no one to buy from. And this is just the start because you will see a lot more creator entrepreneurs in future funded by these big seasoned entrepreneurs or brands, while you will still find it cringe as a founder to post on Instagram. So, if you're a founder or a coach who has been delaying building a distribution on Instagram, check out my page and let's talk in the DM.",
